{"step_index":70,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-03T22:35:05Z","content":"Got Manhwa Outpost data. Now checking ZERO Manhwa Recap.","thinking":"Interesting! Manhwa Outpost data:\n- 405K subs\n- Monthly revenue: $1,045 (!!!)\n- Monthly views: 241,948\n- RPM: $4.32 (same as 1221Recap â€” confirming this is a category-level estimate, NOT channel-specific)\n- Demographics: IDENTICAL gender and age distribution (92% male, same age percentages)\n- Countries: US 34.15%, India 24.67%, Philippines 17.93%, UK 12.54%, Canada 10.71%\n\nWait a minute â€” this is VERY suspicious:\n1. The RPM is IDENTICAL ($4.321428571428571) for both 1221Recap and Manhwa Outpost\n2. Gender split identical, age distribution identical too â€” every percentage matches exactly. This confirms NexLev isn't giving channel-specific data at all; it's applying a category template for \"comics,\" with only views, revenue (derived from views Ã— the same RPM), and country breakdown varying slightly. That's a major data quality issue since the demographics and RPM aren't real per-channel figures. I'm also noting that Manhwa Outpost's 405K subs seems disproportionate to its numbers.\n\nManhwa Outpost is only pulling $1,045/month despite 405K subs â€” barely 0.6 views per subscriber, versus 1221Recap's 284 views per sub at 19.4K subs. That contrast suggests the big established channel is in decline while the smaller one is riding an algorithmic growth spike, a pattern typical of niches where YouTube temporarily favors new channels before shifting attention elsewhere. Now I want to see the ZERO Manhwa Recap data before drawing more conclusions.","tool_calls":[{"name":"manage_task","args":{"Action":"\"status\"","TaskId":"\"294de91b-96ee-4dd8-a251-293eb7aa59a8/task-66\"","toolAction":"\"Checking task status\"","toolSummary":"\"ZERO Manhwa task\""}}]}
