ts.\n24: \n25: ## When to Activate\n26: \n27: - User asks to research any topic in depth\n28: - Competitive analysis, technology evaluation, market sizing\n29: - Trend investigation, \"what's happening with X\n<truncated 22527 bytes>\nation\n435: ```\n436: Layer 1: Fetch the docs landing page or API reference index â†’ identify relevant endpoint/section.\n437: Layer 2: Fetch the specific endpoint documentation page only.\n438: Layer 3: If docs are auto-generated (Swagger/OpenAPI), fetch the schema JSON instead â€” more token-efficient than rendered HTML.\n439: ```\n440: \n441: ### Academic Papers / ArXiv\n442: ```\n443: Layer 1: Fetch abstract page only â†’ determines relevance.\n444: Layer 2: Fetch the PDF if abstract confirms relevance. Read abstract + conclusion + key figures only.\n445: Layer 3: Full paper read â€” only for the 1-2 most critical sources in exhaustive research.\n446: ```\n447: \n448: ---\n449: \n450: ## Anti-Patterns (NEVER Do These)\n451: \n452: | Anti-pattern | Why it's bad | Do this instead |\n453: |---|---|---|\n454: | Searching with raw user query | Vague, irrelevant results | Sharpen query first |\n455: | Dumping raw HTML into context | Noisy, hard to parse | Strip to clean markdown |\n456: | Re-fetching the same URL | Redundant, slows research | Cache in context, reuse |\n457: | Skipping the plan stage | Random, unfocused searching | Break into sub-queries first |\n458: | Reporting without source quality | User can't judge reliability | Always cite sources inline with quality assessment |\n459: | Stopping too early | Incomplete answer | Keep searching until the answer is comprehensive and well-supported |\n460: | Ending with only an apology | User gets nothing | If sources fail or searches return nothing, always produce a partial report with what you found. Mark gaps as \"[Source unavailable â€” partial finding]\" but never end with zero output |\n461: | Synthesizing before all agents report | Missing data creates blind spots | Wait for AL